<misterromdescription>
    <name>Raiden Fighters Jet (Europe, SPI cartridge, pre-flashed)</name>
    <setname>rfjet</setname>
    <rbf>SeibuSPI</rbf>
    <mameversion>0277</mameversion>
    <year>1998</year>
    <manufacturer>Seibu Kaihatsu</manufacturer>
    <players>2</players>
    <joystick>8</joystick>
    <rotation>vertical (cw)</rotation>
    <region>Europe</region>
    <category>Shooter</category>

    <!--
      NOT YET RUN ON HARDWARE, and NOT YET COMPARED AGAINST MAME FRAME BY
      FRAME. What is verified is the RISE11 sprite crypt this set is the only
      user of -- against MAME's own function, in MAME's word order and again in
      the order the fetch walks the words (`make -C sim run-rise11`) -- and the
      part list below, against MAME's ROM_START(rfjet) and against the flash
      image MAME's own flash devices hold.

      THIS SET NEEDS A 64 MB SDRAM MODULE. 37.4 MB of download landing in a
      41 MB map: 24 MB of sprites alone, which is more than the whole of rdfts.

      rfjet is a SXX2C cartridge, NOT the SXX2G single board rfjets. MAME runs
      it on the rdft2 machine config, so the YMF271 stays at 16.9344 MHz and
      the Z80 at 7.159 MHz -- none of the SXX2G clock arithmetic applies here.

      Like rdft2, rfjet keeps its Z80 program in sound1.u0222 and the 386 reads
      it through the sound01 window before releasing the Z80 from reset. Unlike
      rdft2 it lives at 0x44000, not 0x60000, and its LENGTH has never been
      measured. Loader part 16 carries that ROM whole for both sets, so neither
      fact matters here -- the window answers with whatever MAME's region holds
      at the address asked for. See SDR_SND01_BASE in rtl/spi_defs.vh.
    -->

    <!--
      LIKE rdft AND rdft2, THE CARTRIDGE HAS NO SAMPLE ROM: the YMF271 plays out
      of two Intel E28F008SA flash chips the game programs itself on first boot.
      This MRA ships that flash pre-programmed so the several-minute reflash is
      skipped.

      rfjet's updater is the same generation as rdft2's -- a copy job and then a
      compressed one -- so the core decodes the tail during the download with
      rtl/spi_rom_decode.sv, selected by the codec config below. Same codec,
      byte pair encoding over DPCM; only the lengths differ.

        flash[0x000000-0x000003] = maincpu[0x1FFFFC-0x1FFFFF]   region + build
        flash[0x000004-0x189DD4] = pcm-d.u0227[4..]             verbatim
        flash[0x189DD5-0x1E94C9] = decode(sound1.u0222[0..0x41C07])
        flash[0x1E94CA-0x1FFFFF] = 0xFF, erased and never addressed

      Every one of those numbers is read out of the job table in the game's own
      386 program by tools/build_soundflash.py, which reproduces the whole 2 MB
      bit-for-bit and is checked by sha256 against the image MAME's flash
      devices end up holding. NONE of them is copied from rdft2: the copy is
      0x189DD1 bytes against rdft2's 0x17C243 and the decode 0x41C08 against
      0x4C665, and the decoded length in particular cannot be read off the job
      record at all -- that field carries the OUTPUT size.

      The four header bytes are the region lock, not a checksum: a verbatim copy
      of the last four bytes of the 386 program, region byte plus build ID.
      rfjet's is 80 4A 4A 38, one past rdft2's 37 and rdft's 36. A stamp that
      does not match the program makes the game reflash, so this image belongs
      to THIS set's program ROMs and no other region's.
    -->

    <!--
      Part order is significant. rtl/rom_loader.sv walks this list with a fixed
      table -- selected by the mod byte below -- and performs MAME's
      ROM_LOAD24_* / ROM_LOAD32_* byte scatter in hardware, because MRA
      <interleave> cannot express the 3-byte period the Seibu "D + P" graphics
      ROM pairs need. Do not reorder or insert parts without updating the rfjet
      table in rom_loader.sv to match. `make check-mra` checks them against each
      other and against MAME's driver.

      Layout produced in SDRAM (see rtl/spi_defs.vh):
        0x0000000  2 MB   386 program
        0x0200000  256 KB Z80 program -- RAM, NOT loaded here. The 386 fills it
                          through port 0x688 and releases the Z80 with 0x68C.
        0x0240000  192 KB text tiles
        0x0280000  2 MB   YMF271 samples: the pre-programmed flash image
        0x0480000  512 KB sound1.u0222, which the 386 reads through the sound01
                          window, packed one byte per 386 dword
        0x0500000  9 MB   background tiles, two groups 6 MB apart
        0x1100000  24 MB  sprites, three 8 MB plane-pair chunks
    -->

    <!--
      Mod byte 0x05: bit 0 selects the SXX2C cartridge board wiring, bits 3:1
      select set 2 on that board, which is rfjet. rdft2.mra sends 0x03 (same
      board, set 1), rdft.mra 0x01 and rdfts.mra nothing at all.

      The second element is the codec config: pairs of {part index, codec id}.
      Part 15 is the compressed sample tail, and codec 1 is CODEC_BPE_DPCM.
      Parts default to codec 0, a straight copy, so nothing else is listed.
      Ids live in rtl/spi_defs.vh and the decoders in rtl/spi_rom_decode.sv.

      IT MUST COME BEFORE THE index="0" ROM. Main_MiSTer sends the <rom>
      elements in file order, so an index-1 that follows the image arrives
      AFTER the loader has already walked its table -- the download then runs
      against the wrong layout and every part after the first lands wrong.
    -->
    <rom index="1">
        <part>05</part>
        <part>0F 01</part>
    </rom>

    <rom index="0" address="0x30000000" zip="rfjet.zip" md5="none">
        <!-- 386 program: four byte lanes -->
        <part name="prg0.u0211" crc="e5a3b304"/>
        <part name="prg1.u0212" crc="395e6da7"/>
        <part name="prg2.u0221" crc="82f7a57e"/>
        <part name="prg3.u0220" crc="cbdf100d"/>

        <!-- text layer: three byte lanes, and the lane order is 1, 0, 2 -->
        <part name="fix0.u0524" crc="8bc080be"/>
        <part name="fix1.u0518" crc="bded85e7"/>
        <part name="fixp.u0514" crc="015d0748"/>

        <!--
          Background tiles: 9 MB, two D+P groups. The second still starts 6 MB
          in, as rdft2's does, but both of its ROMs are half the size -- the
          group base and the group SIZE are independent and only the base is
          shared with rdft2.
        -->
        <part name="bg-1d.u0543" crc="edfd96da"/>
        <part name="bg-1p.u0544" crc="a4cc4631"/>
        <part name="bg-2d.u0545" crc="731fbb59"/>
        <part name="bg-2p.u0546" crc="03652c25"/>

        <!--
          Sprites: 24 MB in three 8 MB plane-pair chunks, one ROM each, and here
          MAME's order IS the numeric one -- obj-1 is chunk 0. rdft2's is obj3,
          obj2, obj1, so this is not a detail to carry across by eye.

          The loader INTERLEAVES the three chunks so a 16-pixel row is twelve
          contiguous bytes and costs two SDRAM reads instead of three, and it
          also folds in MAME's sprite_reorder(), which RISE11 carries the same
          way RISE10 does. RISE11 then needs the PRE-reorder index of each word
          back at fetch time, because its plane210 sum takes that index as an
          operand; spi_sprite.sv derives it and sim/tb_rise11_decrypt.cpp checks
          the derivation against MAME's final image.
        -->
        <part name="obj-1.u0442" crc="58a59896"/>
        <part name="obj-2.u0443" crc="a121d1e3"/>
        <part name="obj-3.u0444" crc="bc2c0c63"/>

        <!--
          YMF271 sample flash, pre-programmed. Loader part 14: the region stamp
          plus 0x189DD1 bytes of pcm-d.u0227 at identity offset,
          4 + 0x189DD1 = 0x189DD5.
        -->
        <part>80 4A 4A 38</part>
        <part name="pcm-d.u0227" crc="8ee3ff45" offset="0x000004" length="0x189DD1"/>

        <!--
          Loader part 15, CODEC_BPE_DPCM: the compressed tail. 0x41C08 bytes in,
          0x5F6F5 out. This is a LENGTH-LIMITED slice on purpose -- the rest of
          sound1.u0222 is not sample data, it is the Z80 program and the gap
          before it.
        -->
        <part name="sound1.u0222" crc="d4fc3da1" length="0x041C08"/>

        <!--
          Loader part 16: the SAME ROM again, whole. Not a duplicate by
          accident -- the file holds two unrelated things, and one of them is
          rfjet's Z80 program at 0x44000, which the 386 copies into the Z80's
          RAM over port 0x688 before releasing it from reset. Carried whole so
          that neither that offset nor the program's unmeasured length has to
          be right here; spi_cpu.sv answers the whole window from it.
        -->
        <part name="sound1.u0222" crc="d4fc3da1" length="0x080000"/>
    </rom>

    <!--
      Same control layout as rdft2; the names map to joystick bits 4 upwards IN
      ORDER and SeibuSPI.sv decodes them at exactly those positions.
    -->
    <buttons names="Shot,Bomb,Button 3,Start,Coin,Service Coin,Test"
             default="A,B,X,Start,Select,R,L" count="3"/>

    <switches default="00" base="0">
        <dip name="Flip Screen"  bits="0" ids="Off,On"/>
        <dip name="Service Mode" bits="1" ids="Off,On"/>
    </switches>
</misterromdescription>
